{"call_id":"call_0cWjQWe0wEpvnwfgrBz4nQSu","process_id":"2318","turn_id":"019c9e8d-0097-7ef2-85f0-8d834186a523","command":["/usr/bin/bash","-lc","kspec tasks ready --eligible --json"],"cwd":"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"kspec tasks ready --eligible --json"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJF68AWM0F61QBQ957V6T2C1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Session events: Add oversized payload guardrails with externalized blob pointers\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"description\": \"Problem: large ACP updates can inflate JSONL event lines and memory pressure. We need safety limits without losing auditability.\\n\\nScope (steps 3-4):\\n1) Add framing/event safety caps (line/payload size) to prevent single-message heap blowups.\\n2) For oversized payload fields (toolResponse stdout/stderr, rawOutput, large chunk text), write full content to session blob files under .kspec/sessions/<id>/blobs/.\\n3) Store compact pointer objects in events.jsonl (path, bytes, sha256, truncated=true, preview).\\n4) Update session log show/search behavior to surface previews by default and optionally resolve blob content on demand.\\n\\nNon-goal: silently dropping payloads. If payload is externalized, events must keep enough metadata to reconstruct/audit.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@session-events\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 1,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"session-log\",\n      \"memory\",\n      \"reliability\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-27T09:18:10.836Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCT38Z5DFJTVH1DJ860PD4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-test-bootstrap-link-detection\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add tests for bootstrap link detection\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Add unit tests for bootstrap.cjs link detection logic (checkKspecCli). Cover: global kspec not linked, dist not built yet, already linked, symlink resolution on Unix. Extract testable helpers if needed.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@bootstrap-script\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"test\",\n      \"infra\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:07:13.253Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCW71YTXH4JG06QN5CKP5FJ\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-triage-e2e-behavioral-coverage\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add missing behavioral coverage to triage API E2E tests\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-daemon-api\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [\n      \"@task-fix-triage-e2e-ac-gaps\"\n    ],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"testing\",\n      \"e2e\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:44:14.296Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ0YXVQCR1ZY2ZY80G670Q\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Shadow repair: handle missing /bin/sh — use direct git commands instead of spawning shell, fails on \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec shadow repair` (and other shadow branch operations) fails on systems where `/bin/sh` is not available, such as NixOS or fish-only systems. All git operations in `src/parser/shadow.ts` use `execSync()` and `execAsync()` (which is `promisify(exec)`), both of which default to spawning `/bin/sh` to interpret the command string. **Where**: `src/parser/shadow.ts` — all `execSync()` calls (lines ~154, 170, 189, 584, 595, 614) and all `execAsync()` calls (lines ~841, 863, 882, 909, 933, 1007, 1027, 1030, 1179). The `repairShadow()` function at line 1785 is the primary trigger but the issue affects every git-invoking function in the file. **How**: Replace shell-spawning `exec`/`execSync` with `spawnSync`/`spawn` using `{ shell: false }` (or simply omitting shell). Pass git commands as arrays: `spawnSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--git-dir'], { cwd, stdio })`. This avoids needing `/bin/sh` entirely. The `spawn` import already exists at line 12 but is unused for most operations. For `execAsync` calls, use `child_process.execFile` (which does not use a shell by default) wrapped with `promisify`. **Done when**: (1) All `execSync`/`execAsync` calls in `shadow.ts` use `spawnSync`/`execFileAsync` instead of shell-based execution. (2) `kspec shadow repair` succeeds on a system without `/bin/sh`. (3) All existing shadow tests pass. (4) No string interpolation of git commands (eliminates shell injection risk as a bonus).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@shadow-repair-cmd\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:46.139Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVZCFA46XS91H4TP27506\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:31.344Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @shadow-repair-cmd spec. Large scope (15+ functions) but spec exists with ACs. Shell portability fix — replace /bin/sh spawns with direct git commands.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ107WAD90WTAVFFQDW0B1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task patch appears to hang indefinitely — had to kill and use task set + task note as workarou\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task patch @ref` hangs indefinitely when invoked without piped stdin and without `--data`. The command reads from `process.stdin` using a `for await` loop (line 999-1003 in `src/cli/commands/task.ts`) which blocks forever waiting for input when stdin is a TTY. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/task.ts`, lines 993-1004 — the `else` branch of the `if (options.data)` check. When `--data` is not provided, the code does `for await (const chunk of process.stdin)` which never resolves if stdin is a terminal. **How**: Add TTY detection before attempting to read stdin. If `process.stdin.isTTY` is true and `--data` was not provided, print an error message explaining that either `--data <json>` must be provided or JSON must be piped to stdin, then exit with a validation error. Example: `if (!options.data && process.stdin.isTTY) { error('Provide --data or pipe JSON to stdin'); process.exit(EXIT_CODES.VALIDATION_FAILED); }`. Alternatively, add a short timeout on stdin reads. **Done when**: (1) Running `kspec task patch @ref` without `--data` and without piped stdin immediately prints a helpful error and exits. (2) `kspec task patch @ref --data '{...}'` still works. (3) `echo '{...}' | kspec task patch @ref` still works. (4) Batch exec engine (`src/cli/batch-exec.ts`) still works with task patch.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@task-patch\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:47.484Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ11EVH8RKBV4CHZ7R0C89\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Fix @observations slug ambiguity — kspec validate reports 6 errors for duplicate slug. Pre-existing \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec validate` reports 6 reference errors because the slug `observations` maps to two different items: a spec feature (ULID `01KF1XFBK00000000000000000`, type feature in `modules/meta.yaml`) and a skill (ULID `01KJ51SSZ2NVZGXE32NR5TWPS9`, type skill in `kynetic.meta.yaml`). Any `@observations` reference is ambiguous. 6 references in `modules/meta.yaml` (implements fields) and `project.tasks.yaml` (spec_ref) are affected. **Where**: `.kspec/modules/meta.yaml` — the spec feature item with slug `observations`. `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the skill with id `observations`. The slug collision happens in the reference index (`src/parser/refs.ts`) which treats spec slugs and meta item ids as the same namespace. **How**: Rename the skill id from `observations` to something distinct like `observe` or `capture-observations`, OR rename the spec feature slug. The skill id is less entrenched (it was recently created) so renaming it is lower risk. Update: (1) the skill id in `kynetic.meta.yaml`, (2) any skill file references, (3) the rendered skill in `.claude/skills/`. After the rename, `kspec validate` should report 0 reference errors for this slug. Use `kspec batch` for the meta changes. **Done when**: (1) `kspec validate` no longer reports duplicate slug errors for `@observations`. (2) The skill is still functional under its new id. (3) All 6 previously-ambiguous references resolve correctly. (4) `kspec agents generate` still includes the observations skill.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:48.731Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ12GB09XHW1HVNMC7YJC4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph PR merge should auto-delete remote branches after merge. Currently accumulates stale branches \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: After ralph merges PRs via the `pr-review-loop` workflow, remote branches accumulate because the merge step does not include `--delete-branch`. 13 stale branches were found after a single session. **Where**: `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the `pr-review-loop` workflow (id `01KFRX0S3VBZGV72A17E21AB9E`), specifically the merge action step at ~line 750-761 which references `kspec workflow start @pr-review-merge`. Also the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step (~line 297-300) which says `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)`. The release skill at `.claude/skills/release/SKILL.md` line 220 already uses `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch` as a pattern. **How**: Update the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step content in `kynetic.meta.yaml` to explicitly include `--delete-branch` in the `gh pr merge` command guidance. Change the step from `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)` to `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch`. This is a meta YAML change — use `kspec` CLI to update the workflow step, or edit the workflow directly. **Done when**: (1) The `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step includes `--delete-branch` in its guidance. (2) `kspec agents generate` regenerated to reflect the change. (3) After a ralph PR merge, the source branch is deleted from the remote.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:49.803Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ139SP70H2H9GNBH8BM72\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec agents generate should include package version in content hash (or add --force flag) so versio\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec agents generate` skips regeneration when content is unchanged, but the staleness check does not include the package version in the content hash. After a version bump (e.g., 0.6.0 to 0.7.0), the generated file header comment is stale, but the hash matches because version is not part of the hash input. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/agents.ts` — the `computeMetaHash()` function (line 125-154) computes a hash from skills, conventions, workflows, and template sections, but does not include the package version. The `generateFreshnessComment()` function (line 160) uses `version` from the package but this is only in the output, not the hash. The skip logic is at ~line 346-348 where `checkAgentStatus()` compares the stored hash. **How**: Add the package `version` string to the hash input in `computeMetaHash()`. Add `data.version = version` (importing `version` from the package, which is already imported at the top of the file for `generateFreshnessComment`). This way, any version bump automatically invalidates the hash and triggers regeneration. Alternatively or additionally, add a `--force` flag to `kspec agents generate` that bypasses the hash check. **Done when**: (1) `computeMetaHash()` includes the package version in its hash computation. (2) After a version bump, `kspec agents generate` regenerates the file instead of skipping. (3) Existing tests in `tests/agents-instruction-gen.test.ts` updated to reflect the new hash behavior. (4) Optionally: `--force` flag added to bypass the hash check entirely.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@agents-cli\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:50.617Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ142M8AM5HAYQBBG12VRS\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec setup should work without CLAUDECODE=1 env var in non-interactive contexts. Either add a --age\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec setup` requires the `CLAUDECODE=1` environment variable (or `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT` or `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`) to detect the agent type as `claude-code`. In non-interactive contexts like containerized ralph runners, these env vars may not be available, causing `detectAgent()` to return `unknown` and setup to fail or print manual instructions instead of auto-configuring. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/setup.ts` — the `detectAgent()` function (line 82-100) checks `process.env.CLAUDECODE`, `process.env.CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT`, and `process.env.CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. The `registerSetupCommand()` function (line 1638-1655) has options like `--dry-run`, `--author`, `--no-hooks` etc. but no `--agent` flag. The `getSetupStatus()` function (line 774) and the main setup action (line 1345) both call `detectAgent()`. **How**: Add an `--agent <type>` option to `kspec setup` that explicitly specifies the agent type, bypassing `detectAgent()`. Supported values: `claude-code`, `cline`, `cursor`, `windsurf`, `unknown`. When `--agent` is provided, skip detection and use the provided type directly. Also consider a fallback detection: if env vars are absent, check for `~/.claude` directory existence as a weaker signal for `claude-code`. The option should be threaded through to both `runSetup()` and `getSetupStatus()`. **Done when**: (1) `kspec setup --agent claude-code` works without any CLAUDECODE env var set. (2) `kspec setup --status --agent claude-code` reports correct status. (3) `detectAgent()` still works as before when `--agent` is not provided. (4) Invalid `--agent` values are rejected with a clear error. (5) Tests in `tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts` and `tests/setup-pipeline-unification.test.ts` updated.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@enhanced-setup\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:51.412Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1513YJA4PKRTA8CZDE4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task set --priority accepts numbers (1-5) but all documentation, conventions, and plan documen\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task set --priority` and `kspec task add --priority` only accept raw numbers (1-5), but all documentation, conventions, and plan documents use P1/P2/P3 notation. The error message says 'Priority must be a number between 1 and 5' which is confusing when P-notation is used everywhere else. **Where**: `src/cli/validators.ts` — the `parseIntOption()` function (line 36-64) is used for priority parsing. It calls `Number(value)` which rejects `P1` etc. The priority option is defined in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` at lines 608, 827. The `parseIntOption` is called at lines 287-296 (task set) and 759-767 (task add). **How**: Create a `parsePriority()` wrapper function in `src/cli/validators.ts` that first normalizes P-notation before calling `parseIntOption()`. Implementation: strip a leading case-insensitive `P` prefix if present (`value.replace(/^[Pp]/, '')`), then delegate to `parseIntOption()`. Replace all `parseIntOption(options.priority, { min: 1, max: 5, name: 'Priority' })` calls in `task.ts` with `parsePriority(options.priority)`. Update the option description from 'Priority (1-5)' to 'Priority (1-5 or P1-P5)'. Update the error message to mention both formats. **Done when**: (1) `kspec task set @ref --priority P1` works and sets priority to 1. (2) `kspec task add --priority p3` works (case-insensitive). (3) `kspec task set @ref --priority 2` still works (backward compatible). (4) Invalid values like `P6`, `Px`, `0` are rejected with a clear error mentioning both formats. (5) Tests in `tests/cli-validators.test.ts` cover both formats. (6) CLI help text shows accepted format.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:52.387Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1677X9VQ0DGK550NBGZX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Tests fail when run inside ralph loop due to environment contamination: (1) ralph.test.ts KSPEC_RALP\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Three tests fail when run inside a ralph loop because they inherit environment variables from the parent ralph process instead of running in a clean env:\\n\\n1. `tests/ralph.test.ts` line ~618: The test asserts `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` is null for non-ralph child processes, but when tests run inside a ralph loop, the env var leaks from the parent process via `...process.env` spread (line 40).\\n\\n2. `tests/session-create.test.ts`: The `kspecJson()` helper parses stdout as JSON, but when KSPEC_SESSION_ID is inherited from ralph, the session-create command emits an \\\"active session\\\" warning to stderr that contaminates the output parsing.\\n\\n3. `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts`: References a nonexistent `task set-automation` command (the actual command is `task set --automation`).\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `tests/ralph.test.ts` — `runRalph()` helper (lines 27-56), env propagation test (lines 591-619)\\n- `tests/session-create.test.ts` — tests using `kspecJson()` for session creation\\n- `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts` — the test referencing `set-automation`\\n- `tests/helpers/cli.ts` — the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers\\n\\n**How**:\\n- In `runRalph()` and test helpers that spawn child processes, explicitly delete `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` and `KSPEC_SESSION_ID` from the env spread unless the test specifically tests those vars. The pattern at line 601-603 already shows the approach.\\n- Apply the same cleanup to the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers in `tests/helpers/cli.ts` so ALL test invocations are isolated from parent ralph env vars by default.\\n- Fix the `set-automation` reference to use the correct `task set --automation` command syntax.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All three test files pass both standalone (`npm test`) and when run inside a ralph loop\\n- The `kspec()` / `kspecJson()` test helpers strip `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` and `KSPEC_SESSION_ID` from inherited env by default\\n- Tests that need these vars explicitly pass them via the `env` option\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:53.607Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ173NEFK9RFZGRS22VG6M\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Shadow branch index inconsistency: observation @01KJ73ME appeared in search results but failed on ks\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec search` can find observations (e.g., @01KJ73ME) via `loadMetaContext()` which reads the meta.observations array from kynetic.yaml, but `kspec item get` fails for the same reference because the `ReferenceIndex` only indexes spec items and tasks — not meta entities like observations, workflows, conventions, or agents.\\n\\nThis is not a write conflict or index corruption. It is an architectural gap: search and item-get use different resolution paths. Search loads observations from meta context, while item-get only resolves through the ReferenceIndex which does not include meta entities.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` — loads observations via `loadMetaContext()` (lines 241-257)\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` — uses `refIndex.resolve()` which only covers spec items\\n- `src/parser/refs.ts` — `ReferenceIndex` class that builds the resolution index\\n- `src/cli/commands/meta.ts` — has dedicated `meta observe get` subcommand for observations\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo options (pick one):\\n1. **Extend ReferenceIndex** to include meta entities (observations, workflows, agents, conventions) so `item get` can resolve them. This is the deeper fix but has namespace collision risk.\\n2. **Improve error message** in `item get` to suggest `kspec meta observe get @ref` when a ref fails to resolve, since observations have their own dedicated get command. This is lighter and avoids mixing namespaces.\\n\\nOption 2 is recommended: observations already have `kspec meta observe get`, so the real bug is a confusing UX, not missing functionality.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec item get @observation-ref` either resolves the observation or provides a clear error message suggesting `kspec meta observe get @ref`\\n- Alternatively, if extending ReferenceIndex: meta entities are resolvable via `item get` with correct type display\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:54.517Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ17W53F5NN0CWTVSEY53R\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph loop needs iteration-level timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., tool call hanging o\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph loop has no per-iteration timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., a tool call hanging on stdin, like the `kspec batch --commands \\\"\\\"` hang), ralph waits indefinitely. Claude Code's per-bash-command timeout (120s default) does not cover all hang scenarios — an agent can be alive but unresponsive at the ACP protocol level.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` — main loop at line ~1127, the `agent.client.prompt()` call at line ~1373 and the reflection prompt call. These are the two main await points that can hang.\\n- `src/acp/client.ts` — the ACP client that manages the session/prompt lifecycle\\n\\n**How**:\\n- Add a configurable `--iteration-timeout <minutes>` CLI option (default: 20 minutes) to the ralph command registration (line ~868).\\n- Wrap the `agent.client.prompt()` calls in a `Promise.race` with a timeout. On timeout: (1) kill the stuck agent process via `agent.kill()`, (2) log the timeout as a failed iteration, (3) apply the existing retry/failure escalation logic.\\n- The timeout should cover the entire iteration (task-work prompt + optional reflection prompt), not individual prompts, since an iteration is the atomic unit of work.\\n- Consider using `AbortController` / `AbortSignal` if the ACP client supports it, otherwise use `Promise.race` with a timer.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec ralph --iteration-timeout 15` kills a stuck agent after 15 minutes and continues to the next iteration (or exits if max failures reached)\\n- Default timeout is 20 minutes\\n- Timeout events are logged to the session event store with type `iteration.timeout`\\n- Existing retry and failure escalation logic handles timeouts the same as other iteration failures\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:55.301Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQW7RZ6DDS9BNKW8XMDXF4\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:39.935Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. New feature: iteration-level timeout for ralph loop. Spec covers ralph command.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ18VNYYAXETR8WREAJW2X\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec batch --commands '' hangs instead of erroring — empty/missing args should fail fast with a cle\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec batch --commands ''` hangs instead of failing fast. When Commander receives `--commands ''` (empty string), `options.commands` is `''` which is falsy in JavaScript. The source selection logic at `src/cli/commands/batch.ts` line 148 (`else if (options.commands)`) skips the inline path and falls through to `source = { type: 'stdin' }`. The stdin reader at `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 114-124 then blocks forever waiting for input that will never come.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/batch.ts` lines 146-152 — source selection logic\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 100-126 — `readSource()` stdin path\\n\\n**How**:\\nChange the source selection to check `options.commands !== undefined` instead of truthiness:\\n```typescript\\nif (options.file) {\\n  source = { type: 'file', path: options.file };\\n} else if (options.commands !== undefined) {\\n  source = { type: 'inline', json: options.commands };\\n} else {\\n  source = { type: 'stdin' };\\n}\\n```\\nThis way `--commands ''` takes the inline path and gets caught by `parseBatchInput()` which will fail on `JSON.parse('')` with a clear 'Invalid JSON' error.\\n\\nAlternatively, also add early validation in the inline path: if `options.commands` is empty/whitespace, throw `BatchParseError('Empty --commands value')` before attempting parse.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec batch --commands ''` exits immediately with a clear error (not a hang)\\n- `kspec batch --commands ' '` also fails fast\\n- No regression: `kspec batch --commands '[...]'` still works correctly\\n- Add a test case for empty `--commands` input\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:56.309Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ19N46AK8JQT0KQFQV1KH\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"CLI args should accept both kebab-case and underscore variants (e.g. depends-on and depends_on). Cur\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Batch command validation rejects underscore variants of argument names (e.g., `depends_on`) even though kebab-case (`depends-on`) and camelCase (`dependsOn`) are accepted. Agents generating JSON batch payloads naturally use underscores, which is the standard JSON/Python naming convention. The error message suggests the kebab form, creating a frustrating feedback loop.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 180-216 — `kebabToCamel()` and `getKnownArgNames()` functions\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 308-327 — unknown arg validation loop\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 434-496 — `buildCommandArgv()` which also needs to handle underscore keys when building CLI argv\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a `underscoreToKebab()` or `underscoreToCamel()` normalizer alongside `kebabToCamel()`.\\n2. In `getKnownArgNames()`, add underscore variants to the known names set (e.g., for `depends-on`, also add `depends_on`).\\n3. In `buildCommandArgv()`, normalize incoming arg keys before classification — convert underscores to kebab-case before looking up in positionalNameSet and optionMap.\\n4. In the required-args check (lines 330-348), also check underscore variants.\\n\\nA cleaner approach: add a single `normalizeArgKey(key)` function that converts any variant (kebab, camel, underscore) to a canonical form, then use it everywhere.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `{\\\"command\\\": \\\"task set\\\", \\\"args\\\": {\\\"ref\\\": \\\"@task\\\", \\\"depends_on\\\": [\\\"@dep1\\\"]}}` works in batch mode\\n- All three forms accepted: `depends-on`, `dependsOn`, `depends_on`\\n- Same normalization applies to all options, not just depends_on\\n- Add test cases for underscore variant args in batch validation tests\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.124Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1AF125H0FK29HE769R4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph event handler iteration attribution should use session-scoped mapping instead of shared mutabl\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph event handler uses a `sessionIterationMap` (Map<string, number>) to attribute late ACP session updates to the correct iteration. However, the current implementation has a race condition: when a new ACP session starts for iteration N+1, late updates from iteration N's session can arrive after `sessionIterationMap.set(newSessionId, N+1)` but the old session's listener may still fire. The `_sid` closure variable in the `on('update')` handler captures the session ID correctly, but there is no cleanup of old listeners when starting a new session.\\n\\nThe Codex review on PR #484 identified that the shared mutable `sessionIterationMap` approach is fragile — a session-scoped listener pattern would be more robust.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1061 — `sessionIterationMap` declaration\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` lines 1305-1337 — the `on('update')` handler that uses `sessionIterationMap.get(_sid)` for iteration lookup\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1369 — where new sessions are registered in the map\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo approaches (pick one):\\n\\n1. **Session-scoped listeners** (recommended): Instead of one persistent `on('update')` handler, register a new listener per ACP session that captures the iteration number in its closure. Remove the old listener when the session ends or a new one starts. This eliminates the shared mutable map entirely.\\n\\n2. **Cleanup old sessions**: When starting a new ACP session, remove the previous session's entry from `sessionIterationMap` and call `agent.client.off('update', oldHandler)` to prevent stale callbacks. Less clean but simpler delta.\\n\\nBoth approaches should also ensure the translator is reset between iterations (it already creates a fresh translator per retry, but verify per-iteration reset).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- Late updates from a previous ACP session are attributed to the correct iteration (the one that created that session)\\n- No shared mutable state for iteration tracking — either session-scoped closures or explicit cleanup\\n- Existing event logging tests still pass\\n- Event log entries show correct iteration numbers even when sessions overlap\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.953Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVH2H94SY215Y96TMWF0K\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:16.689Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. Ralph internal: session-scoped event listener mapping. Clear scope from enrichment.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\\n\\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures. This is the main fix.\\n2. Alternatively, ensure that `dispatchCommand()` in batch-exec.ts distinguishes between stderr warnings and actual errors when a `BatchExitError(0)` is caught — treat exit code 0 as success regardless of captured output content.\\n3. Consider adding a `warnings` field to `BatchCommandResult` so warnings can be reported without marking the command as failed.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import` with warnings runs successfully in batch mode (no rollback)\\n- Warnings are still visible in batch output (not silenced)\\n- Plan import still fails correctly in batch mode for actual errors (YAML parse errors, missing module, etc.)\\n- Add a test case: batch with a plan import that produces warnings should succeed\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:58.796Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1C22TH9SEXXGBYQT8Y3Z\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new AC via plan import, --update overwrites acceptance_criteria entirely. Need AC-merge strategy: append new AC IDs, preserve existing ones. Encountered during spec-plan for session-log-show ac-10.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:59.586Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ8ZD10EG4Z7MHEAHWBMAT\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:08.769Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec plan import --update` encounters an existing spec, it replaces the entire `acceptance_criteria` array instead of merging. If a spec already has ac-1 through ac-9 and a plan adds ac-10, --update overwrites all ACs with only what the plan document contains, losing ac-1 through ac-9.\\n\\n**Where**: `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 271-303 (the --update branch). The update logic builds a `Partial<SpecItemInput>` and sets `updates.acceptance_criteria = spec.acceptance_criteria` (line 296), passed to `updateSpecItem()` in `src/parser/yaml.ts` line 1522. `updateSpecItem` does direct property assignment (`targetObj[key] = value` at line 1560) with no merge logic.\\n\\n**How**: Implement an AC merge strategy in the update path. When `updates.acceptance_criteria` is set and the existing item already has ACs:\\n1. Index existing ACs by their `id` field (e.g., \\\"ac-1\\\", \\\"ac-2\\\")\\n2. For each AC in the plan update: if an AC with the same `id` exists, replace it; otherwise append it\\n3. Preserve existing ACs whose IDs are not in the update set\\nBest done in `plan-import.ts` before calling `updateSpecItem`, since general updateSpecItem callers may want full replacement semantics.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import --update` with a plan that adds one new AC to an existing spec preserves all pre-existing ACs and appends the new one\\n- A plan that modifies an existing AC (same id) updates that AC in place while preserving others\\n- Tests cover: append-only new AC, update existing AC by id, mixed append+update, no ACs in plan (preserves existing), empty existing ACs (creates all from plan)\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1CTV35WDA9G51NEH943K\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-r\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-ref to read implementation notes, algorithm details, and context. Update task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow to include this step. Plans are the right place for rich implementation context instead of cramming it into task notes.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:00.379Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ9E2V7MF4XRKW0MQQC59X\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:23.804Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When agents start a task that has a `plan_ref` field, they should automatically retrieve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJF68AWM0F61QBQ957V6T2C1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Session events: Add oversized payload guardrails with externalized blob pointers\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"description\": \"Problem: large ACP updates can inflate JSONL event lines and memory pressure. We need safety limits without losing auditability.\\n\\nScope (steps 3-4):\\n1) Add framing/event safety caps (line/payload size) to prevent single-message heap blowups.\\n2) For oversized payload fields (toolResponse stdout/stderr, rawOutput, large chunk text), write full content to session blob files under .kspec/sessions/<id>/blobs/.\\n3) Store compact pointer objects in events.jsonl (path, bytes, sha256, truncated=true, preview).\\n4) Update session log show/search behavior to surface previews by default and optionally resolve blob content on demand.\\n\\nNon-goal: silently dropping payloads. If payload is externalized, events must keep enough metadata to reconstruct/audit.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@session-events\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 1,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"session-log\",\n      \"memory\",\n      \"reliability\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-27T09:18:10.836Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCT38Z5DFJTVH1DJ860PD4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-test-bootstrap-link-detection\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add tests for bootstrap link detection\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Add unit tests for bootstrap.cjs link detection logic (checkKspecCli). Cover: global kspec not linked, dist not built yet, already linked, symlink resolution on Unix. Extract testable helpers if needed.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@bootstrap-script\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"test\",\n      \"infra\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:07:13.253Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCW71YTXH4JG06QN5CKP5FJ\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-triage-e2e-behavioral-coverage\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add missing behavioral coverage to triage API E2E tests\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-daemon-api\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [\n      \"@task-fix-triage-e2e-ac-gaps\"\n    ],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"testing\",\n      \"e2e\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:44:14.296Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ0YXVQCR1ZY2ZY80G670Q\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Shadow repair: handle missing /bin/sh — use direct git commands instead of spawning shell, fails on \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec shadow repair` (and other shadow branch operations) fails on systems where `/bin/sh` is not available, such as NixOS or fish-only systems. All git operations in `src/parser/shadow.ts` use `execSync()` and `execAsync()` (which is `promisify(exec)`), both of which default to spawning `/bin/sh` to interpret the command string. **Where**: `src/parser/shadow.ts` — all `execSync()` calls (lines ~154, 170, 189, 584, 595, 614) and all `execAsync()` calls (lines ~841, 863, 882, 909, 933, 1007, 1027, 1030, 1179). The `repairShadow()` function at line 1785 is the primary trigger but the issue affects every git-invoking function in the file. **How**: Replace shell-spawning `exec`/`execSync` with `spawnSync`/`spawn` using `{ shell: false }` (or simply omitting shell). Pass git commands as arrays: `spawnSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--git-dir'], { cwd, stdio })`. This avoids needing `/bin/sh` entirely. The `spawn` import already exists at line 12 but is unused for most operations. For `execAsync` calls, use `child_process.execFile` (which does not use a shell by default) wrapped with `promisify`. **Done when**: (1) All `execSync`/`execAsync` calls in `shadow.ts` use `spawnSync`/`execFileAsync` instead of shell-based execution. (2) `kspec shadow repair` succeeds on a system without `/bin/sh`. (3) All existing shadow tests pass. (4) No string interpolation of git commands (eliminates shell injection risk as a bonus).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@shadow-repair-cmd\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:46.139Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVZCFA46XS91H4TP27506\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:31.344Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @shadow-repair-cmd spec. Large scope (15+ functions) but spec exists with ACs. Shell portability fix — replace /bin/sh spawns with direct git commands.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ107WAD90WTAVFFQDW0B1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task patch appears to hang indefinitely — had to kill and use task set + task note as workarou\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task patch @ref` hangs indefinitely when invoked without piped stdin and without `--data`. The command reads from `process.stdin` using a `for await` loop (line 999-1003 in `src/cli/commands/task.ts`) which blocks forever waiting for input when stdin is a TTY. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/task.ts`, lines 993-1004 — the `else` branch of the `if (options.data)` check. When `--data` is not provided, the code does `for await (const chunk of process.stdin)` which never resolves if stdin is a terminal. **How**: Add TTY detection before attempting to read stdin. If `process.stdin.isTTY` is true and `--data` was not provided, print an error message explaining that either `--data <json>` must be provided or JSON must be piped to stdin, then exit with a validation error. Example: `if (!options.data && process.stdin.isTTY) { error('Provide --data or pipe JSON to stdin'); process.exit(EXIT_CODES.VALIDATION_FAILED); }`. Alternatively, add a short timeout on stdin reads. **Done when**: (1) Running `kspec task patch @ref` without `--data` and without piped stdin immediately prints a helpful error and exits. (2) `kspec task patch @ref --data '{...}'` still works. (3) `echo '{...}' | kspec task patch @ref` still works. (4) Batch exec engine (`src/cli/batch-exec.ts`) still works with task patch.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@task-patch\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:47.484Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ11EVH8RKBV4CHZ7R0C89\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Fix @observations slug ambiguity — kspec validate reports 6 errors for duplicate slug. Pre-existing \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec validate` reports 6 reference errors because the slug `observations` maps to two different items: a spec feature (ULID `01KF1XFBK00000000000000000`, type feature in `modules/meta.yaml`) and a skill (ULID `01KJ51SSZ2NVZGXE32NR5TWPS9`, type skill in `kynetic.meta.yaml`). Any `@observations` reference is ambiguous. 6 references in `modules/meta.yaml` (implements fields) and `project.tasks.yaml` (spec_ref) are affected. **Where**: `.kspec/modules/meta.yaml` — the spec feature item with slug `observations`. `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the skill with id `observations`. The slug collision happens in the reference index (`src/parser/refs.ts`) which treats spec slugs and meta item ids as the same namespace. **How**: Rename the skill id from `observations` to something distinct like `observe` or `capture-observations`, OR rename the spec feature slug. The skill id is less entrenched (it was recently created) so renaming it is lower risk. Update: (1) the skill id in `kynetic.meta.yaml`, (2) any skill file references, (3) the rendered skill in `.claude/skills/`. After the rename, `kspec validate` should report 0 reference errors for this slug. Use `kspec batch` for the meta changes. **Done when**: (1) `kspec validate` no longer reports duplicate slug errors for `@observations`. (2) The skill is still functional under its new id. (3) All 6 previously-ambiguous references resolve correctly. (4) `kspec agents generate` still includes the observations skill.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:48.731Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ12GB09XHW1HVNMC7YJC4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph PR merge should auto-delete remote branches after merge. Currently accumulates stale branches \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: After ralph merges PRs via the `pr-review-loop` workflow, remote branches accumulate because the merge step does not include `--delete-branch`. 13 stale branches were found after a single session. **Where**: `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the `pr-review-loop` workflow (id `01KFRX0S3VBZGV72A17E21AB9E`), specifically the merge action step at ~line 750-761 which references `kspec workflow start @pr-review-merge`. Also the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step (~line 297-300) which says `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)`. The release skill at `.claude/skills/release/SKILL.md` line 220 already uses `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch` as a pattern. **How**: Update the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step content in `kynetic.meta.yaml` to explicitly include `--delete-branch` in the `gh pr merge` command guidance. Change the step from `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)` to `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch`. This is a meta YAML change — use `kspec` CLI to update the workflow step, or edit the workflow directly. **Done when**: (1) The `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step includes `--delete-branch` in its guidance. (2) `kspec agents generate` regenerated to reflect the change. (3) After a ralph PR merge, the source branch is deleted from the remote.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:49.803Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ139SP70H2H9GNBH8BM72\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec agents generate should include package version in content hash (or add --force flag) so versio\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec agents generate` skips regeneration when content is unchanged, but the staleness check does not include the package version in the content hash. After a version bump (e.g., 0.6.0 to 0.7.0), the generated file header comment is stale, but the hash matches because version is not part of the hash input. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/agents.ts` — the `computeMetaHash()` function (line 125-154) computes a hash from skills, conventions, workflows, and template sections, but does not include the package version. The `generateFreshnessComment()` function (line 160) uses `version` from the package but this is only in the output, not the hash. The skip logic is at ~line 346-348 where `checkAgentStatus()` compares the stored hash. **How**: Add the package `version` string to the hash input in `computeMetaHash()`. Add `data.version = version` (importing `version` from the package, which is already imported at the top of the file for `generateFreshnessComment`). This way, any version bump automatically invalidates the hash and triggers regeneration. Alternatively or additionally, add a `--force` flag to `kspec agents generate` that bypasses the hash check. **Done when**: (1) `computeMetaHash()` includes the package version in its hash computation. (2) After a version bump, `kspec agents generate` regenerates the file instead of skipping. (3) Existing tests in `tests/agents-instruction-gen.test.ts` updated to reflect the new hash behavior. (4) Optionally: `--force` flag added to bypass the hash check entirely.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@agents-cli\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:50.617Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ142M8AM5HAYQBBG12VRS\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec setup should work without CLAUDECODE=1 env var in non-interactive contexts. Either add a --age\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec setup` requires the `CLAUDECODE=1` environment variable (or `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT` or `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`) to detect the agent type as `claude-code`. In non-interactive contexts like containerized ralph runners, these env vars may not be available, causing `detectAgent()` to return `unknown` and setup to fail or print manual instructions instead of auto-configuring. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/setup.ts` — the `detectAgent()` function (line 82-100) checks `process.env.CLAUDECODE`, `process.env.CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT`, and `process.env.CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. The `registerSetupCommand()` function (line 1638-1655) has options like `--dry-run`, `--author`, `--no-hooks` etc. but no `--agent` flag. The `getSetupStatus()` function (line 774) and the main setup action (line 1345) both call `detectAgent()`. **How**: Add an `--agent <type>` option to `kspec setup` that explicitly specifies the agent type, bypassing `detectAgent()`. Supported values: `claude-code`, `cline`, `cursor`, `windsurf`, `unknown`. When `--agent` is provided, skip detection and use the provided type directly. Also consider a fallback detection: if env vars are absent, check for `~/.claude` directory existence as a weaker signal for `claude-code`. The option should be threaded through to both `runSetup()` and `getSetupStatus()`. **Done when**: (1) `kspec setup --agent claude-code` works without any CLAUDECODE env var set. (2) `kspec setup --status --agent claude-code` reports correct status. (3) `detectAgent()` still works as before when `--agent` is not provided. (4) Invalid `--agent` values are rejected with a clear error. (5) Tests in `tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts` and `tests/setup-pipeline-unification.test.ts` updated.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@enhanced-setup\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:51.412Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1513YJA4PKRTA8CZDE4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task set --priority accepts numbers (1-5) but all documentation, conventions, and plan documen\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task set --priority` and `kspec task add --priority` only accept raw numbers (1-5), but all documentation, conventions, and plan documents use P1/P2/P3 notation. The error message says 'Priority must be a number between 1 and 5' which is confusing when P-notation is used everywhere else. **Where**: `src/cli/validators.ts` — the `parseIntOption()` function (line 36-64) is used for priority parsing. It calls `Number(value)` which rejects `P1` etc. The priority option is defined in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` at lines 608, 827. The `parseIntOption` is called at lines 287-296 (task set) and 759-767 (task add). **How**: Create a `parsePriority()` wrapper function in `src/cli/validators.ts` that first normalizes P-notation before calling `parseIntOption()`. Implementation: strip a leading case-insensitive `P` prefix if present (`value.replace(/^[Pp]/, '')`), then delegate to `parseIntOption()`. Replace all `parseIntOption(options.priority, { min: 1, max: 5, name: 'Priority' })` calls in `task.ts` with `parsePriority(options.priority)`. Update the option description from 'Priority (1-5)' to 'Priority (1-5 or P1-P5)'. Update the error message to mention both formats. **Done when**: (1) `kspec task set @ref --priority P1` works and sets priority to 1. (2) `kspec task add --priority p3` works (case-insensitive). (3) `kspec task set @ref --priority 2` still works (backward compatible). (4) Invalid values like `P6`, `Px`, `0` are rejected with a clear error mentioning both formats. (5) Tests in `tests/cli-validators.test.ts` cover both formats. (6) CLI help text shows accepted format.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:52.387Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1677X9VQ0DGK550NBGZX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Tests fail when run inside ralph loop due to environment contamination: (1) ralph.test.ts KSPEC_RALP\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Three tests fail when run inside a ralph loop because they inherit environment variables from the parent ralph process instead of running in a clean env:\\n\\n1. `tests/ralph.test.ts` line ~618: The test asserts `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` is null for non-ralph child processes, but when tests run inside a ralph loop, the env var leaks from the parent process via `...process.env` spread (line 40).\\n\\n2. `tests/session-create.test.ts`: The `kspecJson()` helper parses stdout as JSON, but when KSPEC_SESSION_ID is inherited from ralph, the session-create command emits an \\\"active session\\\" warning to stderr that contaminates the output parsing.\\n\\n3. `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts`: References a nonexistent `task set-automation` command (the actual command is `task set --automation`).\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `tests/ralph.test.ts` — `runRalph()` helper (lines 27-56), env propagation test (lines 591-619)\\n- `tests/session-create.test.ts` — tests using `kspecJson()` for session creation\\n- `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts` — the test referencing `set-automation`\\n- `tests/helpers/cli.ts` — the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers\\n\\n**How**:\\n- In `runRalph()` and test helpers that spawn child processes, explicitly delete `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` and `KSPEC_SESSION_ID` from the env spread unless the test specifically tests those vars. The pattern at line 601-603 already shows the approach.\\n- Apply the same cleanup to the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers in `tests/helpers/cli.ts` so ALL test invocations are isolated from parent ralph env vars by default.\\n- Fix the `set-automation` reference to use the correct `task set --automation` command syntax.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All three test files pass both standalone (`npm test`) and when run inside a ralph loop\\n- The `kspec()` / `kspecJson()` test helpers strip `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` and `KSPEC_SESSION_ID` from inherited env by default\\n- Tests that need these vars explicitly pass them via the `env` option\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:53.607Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ173NEFK9RFZGRS22VG6M\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Shadow branch index inconsistency: observation @01KJ73ME appeared in search results but failed on ks\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec search` can find observations (e.g., @01KJ73ME) via `loadMetaContext()` which reads the meta.observations array from kynetic.yaml, but `kspec item get` fails for the same reference because the `ReferenceIndex` only indexes spec items and tasks — not meta entities like observations, workflows, conventions, or agents.\\n\\nThis is not a write conflict or index corruption. It is an architectural gap: search and item-get use different resolution paths. Search loads observations from meta context, while item-get only resolves through the ReferenceIndex which does not include meta entities.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` — loads observations via `loadMetaContext()` (lines 241-257)\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` — uses `refIndex.resolve()` which only covers spec items\\n- `src/parser/refs.ts` — `ReferenceIndex` class that builds the resolution index\\n- `src/cli/commands/meta.ts` — has dedicated `meta observe get` subcommand for observations\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo options (pick one):\\n1. **Extend ReferenceIndex** to include meta entities (observations, workflows, agents, conventions) so `item get` can resolve them. This is the deeper fix but has namespace collision risk.\\n2. **Improve error message** in `item get` to suggest `kspec meta observe get @ref` when a ref fails to resolve, since observations have their own dedicated get command. This is lighter and avoids mixing namespaces.\\n\\nOption 2 is recommended: observations already have `kspec meta observe get`, so the real bug is a confusing UX, not missing functionality.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec item get @observation-ref` either resolves the observation or provides a clear error message suggesting `kspec meta observe get @ref`\\n- Alternatively, if extending ReferenceIndex: meta entities are resolvable via `item get` with correct type display\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:54.517Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ17W53F5NN0CWTVSEY53R\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph loop needs iteration-level timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., tool call hanging o\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph loop has no per-iteration timeout. When a spawned agent gets stuck (e.g., a tool call hanging on stdin, like the `kspec batch --commands \\\"\\\"` hang), ralph waits indefinitely. Claude Code's per-bash-command timeout (120s default) does not cover all hang scenarios — an agent can be alive but unresponsive at the ACP protocol level.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` — main loop at line ~1127, the `agent.client.prompt()` call at line ~1373 and the reflection prompt call. These are the two main await points that can hang.\\n- `src/acp/client.ts` — the ACP client that manages the session/prompt lifecycle\\n\\n**How**:\\n- Add a configurable `--iteration-timeout <minutes>` CLI option (default: 20 minutes) to the ralph command registration (line ~868).\\n- Wrap the `agent.client.prompt()` calls in a `Promise.race` with a timeout. On timeout: (1) kill the stuck agent process via `agent.kill()`, (2) log the timeout as a failed iteration, (3) apply the existing retry/failure escalation logic.\\n- The timeout should cover the entire iteration (task-work prompt + optional reflection prompt), not individual prompts, since an iteration is the atomic unit of work.\\n- Consider using `AbortController` / `AbortSignal` if the ACP client supports it, otherwise use `Promise.race` with a timer.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec ralph --iteration-timeout 15` kills a stuck agent after 15 minutes and continues to the next iteration (or exits if max failures reached)\\n- Default timeout is 20 minutes\\n- Timeout events are logged to the session event store with type `iteration.timeout`\\n- Existing retry and failure escalation logic handles timeouts the same as other iteration failures\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:55.301Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQW7RZ6DDS9BNKW8XMDXF4\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:39.935Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. New feature: iteration-level timeout for ralph loop. Spec covers ralph command.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ18VNYYAXETR8WREAJW2X\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec batch --commands '' hangs instead of erroring — empty/missing args should fail fast with a cle\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec batch --commands ''` hangs instead of failing fast. When Commander receives `--commands ''` (empty string), `options.commands` is `''` which is falsy in JavaScript. The source selection logic at `src/cli/commands/batch.ts` line 148 (`else if (options.commands)`) skips the inline path and falls through to `source = { type: 'stdin' }`. The stdin reader at `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 114-124 then blocks forever waiting for input that will never come.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/batch.ts` lines 146-152 — source selection logic\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 100-126 — `readSource()` stdin path\\n\\n**How**:\\nChange the source selection to check `options.commands !== undefined` instead of truthiness:\\n```typescript\\nif (options.file) {\\n  source = { type: 'file', path: options.file };\\n} else if (options.commands !== undefined) {\\n  source = { type: 'inline', json: options.commands };\\n} else {\\n  source = { type: 'stdin' };\\n}\\n```\\nThis way `--commands ''` takes the inline path and gets caught by `parseBatchInput()` which will fail on `JSON.parse('')` with a clear 'Invalid JSON' error.\\n\\nAlternatively, also add early validation in the inline path: if `options.commands` is empty/whitespace, throw `BatchParseError('Empty --commands value')` before attempting parse.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec batch --commands ''` exits immediately with a clear error (not a hang)\\n- `kspec batch --commands ' '` also fails fast\\n- No regression: `kspec batch --commands '[...]'` still works correctly\\n- Add a test case for empty `--commands` input\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:56.309Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ19N46AK8JQT0KQFQV1KH\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"CLI args should accept both kebab-case and underscore variants (e.g. depends-on and depends_on). Cur\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Batch command validation rejects underscore variants of argument names (e.g., `depends_on`) even though kebab-case (`depends-on`) and camelCase (`dependsOn`) are accepted. Agents generating JSON batch payloads naturally use underscores, which is the standard JSON/Python naming convention. The error message suggests the kebab form, creating a frustrating feedback loop.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 180-216 — `kebabToCamel()` and `getKnownArgNames()` functions\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 308-327 — unknown arg validation loop\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 434-496 — `buildCommandArgv()` which also needs to handle underscore keys when building CLI argv\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a `underscoreToKebab()` or `underscoreToCamel()` normalizer alongside `kebabToCamel()`.\\n2. In `getKnownArgNames()`, add underscore variants to the known names set (e.g., for `depends-on`, also add `depends_on`).\\n3. In `buildCommandArgv()`, normalize incoming arg keys before classification — convert underscores to kebab-case before looking up in positionalNameSet and optionMap.\\n4. In the required-args check (lines 330-348), also check underscore variants.\\n\\nA cleaner approach: add a single `normalizeArgKey(key)` function that converts any variant (kebab, camel, underscore) to a canonical form, then use it everywhere.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `{\\\"command\\\": \\\"task set\\\", \\\"args\\\": {\\\"ref\\\": \\\"@task\\\", \\\"depends_on\\\": [\\\"@dep1\\\"]}}` works in batch mode\\n- All three forms accepted: `depends-on`, `dependsOn`, `depends_on`\\n- Same normalization applies to all options, not just depends_on\\n- Add test cases for underscore variant args in batch validation tests\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.124Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1AF125H0FK29HE769R4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph event handler iteration attribution should use session-scoped mapping instead of shared mutabl\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Ralph event handler uses a `sessionIterationMap` (Map<string, number>) to attribute late ACP session updates to the correct iteration. However, the current implementation has a race condition: when a new ACP session starts for iteration N+1, late updates from iteration N's session can arrive after `sessionIterationMap.set(newSessionId, N+1)` but the old session's listener may still fire. The `_sid` closure variable in the `on('update')` handler captures the session ID correctly, but there is no cleanup of old listeners when starting a new session.\\n\\nThe Codex review on PR #484 identified that the shared mutable `sessionIterationMap` approach is fragile — a session-scoped listener pattern would be more robust.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1061 — `sessionIterationMap` declaration\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` lines 1305-1337 — the `on('update')` handler that uses `sessionIterationMap.get(_sid)` for iteration lookup\\n- `src/cli/commands/ralph.ts` line 1369 — where new sessions are registered in the map\\n\\n**How**:\\nTwo approaches (pick one):\\n\\n1. **Session-scoped listeners** (recommended): Instead of one persistent `on('update')` handler, register a new listener per ACP session that captures the iteration number in its closure. Remove the old listener when the session ends or a new one starts. This eliminates the shared mutable map entirely.\\n\\n2. **Cleanup old sessions**: When starting a new ACP session, remove the previous session's entry from `sessionIterationMap` and call `agent.client.off('update', oldHandler)` to prevent stale callbacks. Less clean but simpler delta.\\n\\nBoth approaches should also ensure the translator is reset between iterations (it already creates a fresh translator per retry, but verify per-iteration reset).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- Late updates from a previous ACP session are attributed to the correct iteration (the one that created that session)\\n- No shared mutable state for iteration tracking — either session-scoped closures or explicit cleanup\\n- Existing event logging tests still pass\\n- Event log entries show correct iteration numbers even when sessions overlap\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@cli-ralph\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:57.953Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVH2H94SY215Y96TMWF0K\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:16.689Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @cli-ralph spec. Ralph internal: session-scoped event listener mapping. Clear scope from enrichment.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\\n\\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures. This is the main fix.\\n2. Alternatively, ensure that `dispatchCommand()` in batch-exec.ts distinguishes between stderr warnings and actual errors when a `BatchExitError(0)` is caught — treat exit code 0 as success regardless of captured output content.\\n3. Consider adding a `warnings` field to `BatchCommandResult` so warnings can be reported without marking the command as failed.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import` with warnings runs successfully in batch mode (no rollback)\\n- Warnings are still visible in batch output (not silenced)\\n- Plan import still fails correctly in batch mode for actual errors (YAML parse errors, missing module, etc.)\\n- Add a test case: batch with a plan import that produces warnings should succeed\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:58.796Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1C22TH9SEXXGBYQT8Y3Z\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Plan import --update replaces all ACs instead of merging. When extending an existing spec with a new AC via plan import, --update overwrites acceptance_criteria entirely. Need AC-merge strategy: append new AC IDs, preserve existing ones. Encountered during spec-plan for session-log-show ac-10.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@plan-import\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:59.586Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ8ZD10EG4Z7MHEAHWBMAT\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:08.769Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec plan import --update` encounters an existing spec, it replaces the entire `acceptance_criteria` array instead of merging. If a spec already has ac-1 through ac-9 and a plan adds ac-10, --update overwrites all ACs with only what the plan document contains, losing ac-1 through ac-9.\\n\\n**Where**: `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 271-303 (the --update branch). The update logic builds a `Partial<SpecItemInput>` and sets `updates.acceptance_criteria = spec.acceptance_criteria` (line 296), passed to `updateSpecItem()` in `src/parser/yaml.ts` line 1522. `updateSpecItem` does direct property assignment (`targetObj[key] = value` at line 1560) with no merge logic.\\n\\n**How**: Implement an AC merge strategy in the update path. When `updates.acceptance_criteria` is set and the existing item already has ACs:\\n1. Index existing ACs by their `id` field (e.g., \\\"ac-1\\\", \\\"ac-2\\\")\\n2. For each AC in the plan update: if an AC with the same `id` exists, replace it; otherwise append it\\n3. Preserve existing ACs whose IDs are not in the update set\\nBest done in `plan-import.ts` before calling `updateSpecItem`, since general updateSpecItem callers may want full replacement semantics.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec plan import --update` with a plan that adds one new AC to an existing spec preserves all pre-existing ACs and appends the new one\\n- A plan that modifies an existing AC (same id) updates that AC in place while preserving others\\n- Tests cover: append-only new AC, update existing AC by id, mixed append+update, no ACs in plan (preserves existing), empty existing ACs (creates all from plan)\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1CTV35WDA9G51NEH943K\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-r\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Agents should check plan_ref when starting tasks. If a task has plan_ref, run kspec plan get @plan-ref to read implementation notes, algorithm details, and context. Update task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow to include this step. Plans are the right place for rich implementation context instead of cramming it into task notes.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:00.379Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ9E2V7MF4XRKW0MQQC59X\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:23.804Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When agents start a task that has a `plan_ref` field, they should automatically retrieve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":882551069},"formatted_output":"Total output lines: 611\n\n[\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJF68AWM0F61QBQ957V6T2C1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Session events: Add oversized payload guardrails with externalized blob pointers\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"description\": \"Problem: large ACP updates can inflate JSONL event lines and memory pressure. We need safety limits without losing auditability.\\n\\nScope (steps 3-4):\\n1) Add framing/event safety caps (line/payload size) to prevent single-message heap blowups.\\n2) For oversized payload fields (toolResponse stdout/stderr, rawOutput, large chunk text), write full content to session blob files under .kspec/sessions/<id>/blobs/.\\n3) Store compact pointer objects in events.jsonl (path, bytes, sha256, truncated=true, preview).\\n4) Update session log show/search behavior to surface previews by default and optionally resolve blob content on demand.\\n\\nNon-goal: silently dropping payloads. If payload is externalized, events must keep enough metadata to reconstruct/audit.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@session-events\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 1,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"session-log\",\n      \"memory\",\n      \"reliability\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-27T09:18:10.836Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCT38Z5DFJTVH1DJ860PD4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-test-bootstrap-link-detection\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add tests for bootstrap link detection\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Add unit tests for bootstrap.cjs link detection logic (checkKspecCli). Cover: global kspec not linked, dist not built yet, already linked, symlink resolution on Unix. Extract testable helpers if needed.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@bootstrap-script\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"test\",\n      \"infra\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:07:13.253Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJCW71YTXH4JG06QN5CKP5FJ\",\n    \"slugs\": [\n      \"task-triage-e2e-behavioral-coverage\"\n    ],\n    \"title\": \"Add missing behavioral coverage to triage API E2E tests\",\n    \"type\": \"bug\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-daemon-api\",\n    \"meta_ref\": null,\n    \"plan_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [\n      \"@task-fix-triage-e2e-ac-gaps\"\n    ],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"testing\",\n      \"e2e\"\n    ],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T11:44:14.296Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ0YXVQCR1ZY2ZY80G670Q\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Shadow repair: handle missing /bin/sh — use direct git commands instead of spawning shell, fails on \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec shadow repair` (and other shadow branch operations) fails on systems where `/bin/sh` is not available, such as NixOS or fish-only systems. All git operations in `src/parser/shadow.ts` use `execSync()` and `execAsync()` (which is `promisify(exec)`), both of which default to spawning `/bin/sh` to interpret the command string. **Where**: `src/parser/shadow.ts` — all `execSync()` calls (lines ~154, 170, 189, 584, 595, 614) and all `execAsync()` calls (lines ~841, 863, 882, 909, 933, 1007, 1027, 1030, 1179). The `repairShadow()` function at line 1785 is the primary trigger but the issue affects every git-invoking function in the file. **How**: Replace shell-spawning `exec`/`execSync` with `spawnSync`/`spawn` using `{ shell: false }` (or simply omitting shell). Pass git commands as arrays: `spawnSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--git-dir'], { cwd, stdio })`. This avoids needing `/bin/sh` entirely. The `spawn` import already exists at line 12 but is unused for most operations. For `execAsync` calls, use `child_process.execFile` (which does not use a shell by default) wrapped with `promisify`. **Done when**: (1) All `execSync`/`execAsync` calls in `shadow.ts` use `spawnSync`/`execFileAsync` instead of shell-based execution. (2) `kspec shadow repair` succeeds on a system without `/bin/sh`. (3) All existing shadow tests pass. (4) No string interpolation of git commands (eliminates shell injection risk as a bonus).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@shadow-repair-cmd\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:46.139Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVZCFA46XS91H4TP27506\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:31.344Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @shadow-repair-cmd spec. Large scope (15+ functions) but spec exists with ACs. Shell portability fix — replace /bin/sh spawns with direct git commands.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ107WAD90WTAVFFQDW0B1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task patch appears to hang indefinitely — had to kill and use task set + task note as workarou\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task patch @ref` hangs indefinitely when invoked without piped stdin and without `--data`. The command reads from `process.stdin` using a `for await` loop (line 999-1003 in `src/cli/commands/task.ts`) which blocks forever waiting for input when stdin is a TTY. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/task.ts`, lines 993-1004 — the `else` branch of the `if (options.data)` check. When `--data` is not provided, the code does `for await (const chunk of process.stdin)` which never resolves if stdin is a terminal. **How**: Add TTY detection before attempting to read stdin. If `process.stdin.isTTY` is true and `--data` was not provided, print an error message explaining that either `--data <json>` must be provided or JSON must be piped to stdin, then exit with a validation error. Example: `if (!options.data && process.stdin.isTTY) { error('Provide --data or pipe JSON to stdin'); process.exit(EXIT_CODES.VALIDATION_FAILED); }`. Alternatively, add a short timeout on stdin reads. **Done when**: (1) Running `kspec task patch @ref` without `--data` and without piped stdin immediately prints a helpful error and exits. (2) `kspec task patch @ref --data '{...}'` still works. (3) `echo '{...}' | kspec task patch @ref` still works. (4) Batch exec engine (`src/cli/batch-exec.ts`) still works with task patch.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@task-patch\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:47.484Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ11EVH8RKBV4CHZ7R0C89\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Fix @observations slug ambiguity — kspec validate reports 6 errors for duplicate slug. Pre-existing \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec validate` reports 6 reference errors because the slug `observations` maps to two different items: a spec feature (ULID `01KF1XFBK00000000000000000`, type feature in `modules/meta.yaml`) and a skill (ULID `01KJ51SSZ2NVZGXE32NR5TWPS9`, type skill in `kynetic.meta.yaml`). Any `@observations` reference is ambiguous. 6 references in `modules/meta.yaml` (implements fields) and `project.tasks.yaml` (spec_ref) are affected. **Where**: `.kspec/modules/meta.yaml` — the spec feature item with slug `observations`. `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the skill with id `observations`. The slug collision happens in the reference index (`src/parser/refs.ts`) which treats spec slugs and meta item ids as the same namespace. **How**: Rename the skill id from `observations` to something distinct like `observe` or `capture-observations`, OR rename the spec feature slug. The skill id is less entrenched (it was recently created) so renaming it is lower risk. Update: (1) the skill id in `kynetic.meta.yaml`, (2) any skill file references, (3) the rendered skill in `.claude/skills/`. After the rename, `kspec validate` should report 0 reference errors for this slug. Use `kspec batch` for the meta changes. **Done when**: (1) `kspec validate` no longer reports duplicate slug errors for `@observations`. (2) The skill is still functional under its new id. (3) All 6 previously-ambiguous references resolve correctly. (4) `kspec agents generate` still includes the observations skill.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:48.731Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ12GB09XHW1HVNMC7YJC4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Ralph PR merge should auto-delete remote branches after merge. Currently accumulates stale branches \",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: After ralph merges PRs via the `pr-review-loop` workflow, remote branches accumulate because the merge step does not include `--delete-branch`. 13 stale branches were found after a single session. **Where**: `.kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml` — the `pr-review-loop` workflow (id `01KFRX0S3VBZGV72A17E21AB9E`), specifically the merge action step at ~line 750-761 which references `kspec workflow start @pr-review-merge`. Also the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step (~line 297-300) which says `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)`. The release skill at `.claude/skills/release/SKILL.md` line 220 already uses `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch` as a pattern. **How**: Update the `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step content in `kynetic.meta.yaml` to explicitly include `--delete-branch` in the `gh pr merge` command guidance. Change the step from `gh pr merge (prefer merge commit to preserve trailers)` to `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch`. This is a meta YAML change — use `kspec` CLI to update the workflow step, or edit the workflow directly. **Done when**: (1) The `pr-review-merge` workflow merge step includes `--delete-branch` in its guidance. (2) `kspec agents generate` regenerated to reflect the change. (3) After a ralph PR merge, the source branch is deleted from the remote.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": null,\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:49.803Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ139SP70H2H9GNBH8BM72\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec agents generate should include package version in content hash (or add --force flag) so versio\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec agents generate` skips regeneration when content is unchanged, but the staleness check does not include the package version in the content hash. After a version bump (e.g., 0.6.0 to 0.7.0), the generated file header comment is stale, but the hash matches because version is not part of the hash input. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/agents.ts` — the `computeMetaHash()` function (line 125-154) computes a hash from skills, conventions, workflows, and template sections, but does not include the package version. The `generateFreshnessComment()` function (line 160) uses `version` from the package but this is only in the output, not the hash. The skip logic is at ~line 346-348 where `checkAgentStatus()` compares the stored hash. **How**: Add the package `version` string to the hash input in `computeMetaHash()`. Add `data.version = version` (importing `version` from the package, which is already imported at the top of the file for `generateFreshnessComment`). This way, any version bump automatically invalidates the hash and triggers regeneration. Alternatively or additionally, add a `--force` flag to `kspec agents generate` that bypasses the hash check. **Done when**: (1) `computeMetaHash()` includes the package version in its hash computation. (2) After a version bump, `kspec agents generate` regenerates the file instead of skipping. (3) Existing tests in `tests/agents-instruction-gen.test.ts` updated to reflect the new hash behavior. (4) Optionally: `--force` flag added to bypass the hash check entirely.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@agents-cli\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:50.617Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ142M8AM5HAYQBBG12VRS\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec setup should work without CLAUDECODE=1 env var in non-interactive contexts. Either add a --age\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec setup` requires the `CLAUDECODE=1` environment variable (or `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT` or `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`) to detect the agent type as `claude-code`. In non-interactive contexts like containerized ralph runners, these env vars may not be available, causing `detectAgent()` to return `unknown` and setup to fail or print manual instructions instead of auto-configuring. **Where**: `src/cli/commands/setup.ts` — the `detectAgent()` function (line 82-100) checks `process.env.CLAUDECODE`, `process.env.CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT`, and `process.env.CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. The `registerSetupCommand()` function (line 1638-1655) has options like `--dry-run`, `--author`, `--no-hooks` etc. but no `--agent` flag. The `getSetupStatus()` function (line 774) and the main setup action (line 1345) both call `detectAgent()`. **How**: Add an `--agent <type>` option to `kspec setup` that explicitly specifies the agent type, bypassing `detectAgent()`. Supported values: `claude-code`, `cline`, `cursor`, `windsurf`, `unknown`. When `--agent` is provided, skip detection and use the provided type directly. Also consider a fallback detection: if env vars are absent, check for `~/.claude` directory existence as a weaker signal for `claude-code`. The option should be threaded through to both `runSetup()` and `getSetupStatus()`. **Done when**: (1) `kspec setup --agent claude-code` works without any CLAUDECODE env var set. (2) `kspec setup --status --agent claude-code` reports correct status. (3) `detectAgent()` still works as before when `--agent` is not provided. (4) Invalid `--agent` values are rejected with a clear error. (5) Tests in `tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts` and `tests/setup-pipeline-unification.test.ts` updated.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@enhanced-setup\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:51.412Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1513YJA4PKRTA8CZDE4P\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"kspec task set --priority accepts numbers (1-5) but all documentation, conventions, and plan documen\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: `kspec task set --priority` and `kspec task add --priority` only accept raw numbers (1-5), but all documentation, conventions, and plan documents use P1/P2/P3 notation. The error message says 'Priority must be a number between 1 and 5' which is confusing when P-notation is used everywhere else. **Where**: `src/cli/validators.ts` — the `parseIntOption()` function (line 36-64) is used for priority parsing. It calls `Number(value)` which rejects `P1` etc. The priority option is defined in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` at lines 608, 827. The `parseIntOption` is called at lines 287-296 (task set) and 759-767 (task add). **How**: Create a `parsePriority()` wrapper function in `src/cli/validators.ts` that first normalizes P-notation before calling `parseIntOption()`. Implementation: strip a leading case-insensitive `P` prefix if present (`value.replace(/^[Pp]/, '')`), then delegate to `parseIntOption()`. Replace all `parseIntOption(options.priority, { min: 1, max: 5, name: 'Priority' })` calls in `task.ts` with `parsePriority(options.priority)`. Update the option description from 'Priority (1-5)' to 'Priority (1-5 or P1-P5)'. Update the error message to mention both formats. **Done when**: (1) `kspec task set @ref --priority P1` works and sets priority to 1. (2) `kspec task add --priority p3` works (case-insensitive). (3) `kspec task set @ref --priority 2` still works (backward compatible). (4) Invalid values like `P6`, `Px`, `0` are rejected with a clear error mentioning both formats. (5) Tests in `tests/cli-validators.test.ts` cover both formats. (6) CLI help text shows accepted format.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:32:52.387Z\",\n    \"notes\": [],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1677X9VQ0DGK550NBGZX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Tests fail when run inside ralph loop due to environment contamination: (1) ralph.test.ts KSPEC_RALP\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"**What**: Three tests fail when run inside a ralph loop because they inherit environment variables from the parent ralph process instead of running in a clean env:\\n\\n1. `tests/ralph.test.ts` line ~618: The test asserts `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` is null for non-ralph child processes, but when tests run inside a ralph loop, the env var leaks from the parent process via `...process.env` spread (line 40).\\n\\n2. `tests/session-create.test.ts`: The `kspecJson()` helper parses stdout as JSON, but when KSPEC_SESSION_ID is inherited from ralph, the session-create command emits an \\\"active session\\\" warning to stderr that contaminates the output parsing.\\n\\n3. `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts`: References a nonexistent `task set-automation` command (the actual command is `task set --automation`).\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `tests/ralph.test.ts` — `runRalph()` helper (lines 27-56), env propagation test (lines 591-619)\\n- `tests/session-create.test.ts` — tests using `kspecJson()` for session creation\\n- `tests/ralph/session-budget-integration.test.ts` — the test referencing `set-automation`\\n- `tests/helpers/cli.ts` — the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers\\n\\n**How**:\\n- In `runRalph()` and test helpers that spawn child processes, explicitly delete `KSPEC_RALPH_SESSION` and `KSPEC_SESSION_ID` from the env spread unless the test specifically tests those vars. The pattern at line 601-603 already shows the approach.\\n- Apply the same cleanup to the `kspec()` and `kspecJson()` helpers in `tests/helpers/cli.ts` so ALL test invocations are isolated from parent ralph env vars by default.\\n- Fix the `set-automation` reference to use the correct `task set --automation` command syntax.\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All three test files pass both standalone (`npm test`) and when …5011 tokens truncated…ve the plan to get implementation notes, algorithm details, and design context. Currently the task-work skill and task-work-loop workflow do not mention checking plan_ref. Plans are the intended home for rich implementation context rather than cramming it into task descriptions or notes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` — Step 3 \\\"Start Task\\\" section (line 86-90) needs a substep: \\\"If task has plan_ref, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes\\\"\\n- Task-work-loop workflow definition in `.kspec/` (the workflow YAML) — add the same step\\n- `src/cli/commands/task.ts` — the `task get` output already shows `plan_ref` when present; the `task start` command could optionally print a hint like \\\"This task has a plan: run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` for implementation context\\\"\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Update `templates/skills/task-work/SKILL.md` step 3 to add: \\\"If `kspec task get @ref` shows a `plan_ref`, run `kspec plan get @plan-ref` to read implementation notes and design context before starting work.\\\"\\n2. Update the task-work-loop workflow to include the same check in its automated flow\\n3. Optionally enhance `kspec task start` in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` to print an info message when the started task has a non-null plan_ref\\n4. Run `kspec agents generate` after template changes\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- The task-work skill documents checking plan_ref when starting tasks\\n- The task-work-loop workflow includes plan context retrieval\\n- Agents following the skill will automatically get plan context when a task has plan_ref\\n- `kspec agents generate` produces updated kspec-agents.md\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQVPYHQ56GVPJH0EKJ6TWV\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:22.705Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Infra: Workflow/skill documentation change. Agents check plan_ref when starting tasks. No code change needed, skill template update.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1DQMCBX32KDVWBJBF8W1\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"ULID short ref ambiguity in batch operations: when two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g. 01KJ4SNE9 and 01KJ4SNEC), short refs like @01KJ4SNE fail. kspec already has a shortest-unique-ID utility — this should be used anywhere refs are displayed (triage list, task list, inbox list, promote result_ref, etc.) so that presented refs are always unambiguous and copy-pasteable into subsequent commands.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@batch-exec\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:01.300Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQA0MV956KKZREMG980AE6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:37:42.812Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When two ULIDs share a prefix (e.g., 01KJ4SNE9... and 01KJ4SNEC...), the default 8-character short ref `@01KJ4SNE` is ambiguous and fails to resolve. The `ReferenceIndex.shortUlid()` method (in `src/parser/refs.ts` line 310) already computes the minimum unique prefix, but many places in the codebase use a hardcoded `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` instead. There are 69 occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` across 19 files.\\n\\n**Where**: All places that format ULID refs for display need to use `shortUlid()` instead of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)`. Key files with hardcoded slicing:\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` (14 occurrences) — triage list, record, act, get, start\\n- `src/cli/commands/inbox.ts` (9 occurrences) — inbox list, add, promote\\n- `src/cli/commands/item.ts` (6 occurrences) — item list, get, add\\n- `src/cli/commands/plan.ts` (9 occurrences) — plan list, get\\n- `src/cli/commands/search.ts` (4 occurrences) — search results\\n- `src/cli/commands/session/context.ts` (4 occurrences) — session context display\\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` (1 occurrence) — promote result ref\\n- `src/parser/validate.ts` (8 occurrences) — validation output\\n- Several other files with 1-3 occurrences each\\n\\nThe `ReferenceIndex` is already available in most of these contexts (it's built during `initContext()` or `buildIndexes()`).\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. For each file, replace `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` with `index.shortUlid(_ulid)` where a ReferenceIndex is in scope\\n2. For files where no index is available, either thread the index through or create a lightweight shortening utility\\n3. For triage records (which aren't indexed in the main ReferenceIndex), consider building a local index or a dedicated triage ULID shortener\\n4. Ensure that refs displayed by any list/get/result command are always copy-pasteable into subsequent commands without ambiguity\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- No occurrences of `_ulid.slice(0, 8)` remain in display-facing code paths\\n- All displayed refs (triage list, task list, inbox list, search results, promote result_ref, etc.) use `shortUlid()` or equivalent\\n- A test with two items sharing an 8-character ULID prefix shows that displayed refs are still unique and resolvable\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1EKVV4V60V7WT6DCK3H3\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently pro\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Triage promote action should optionally delete the inbox item after creating the task. Currently promoted items stay in inbox requiring manual cleanup. Consider --consume flag or making deletion the default for promote (with snapshot preserved in triage record).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@triage-cli-commands\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:02.203Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQAHM5SX18W9F5V0ET02G6\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:00.197Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: When `kspec triage act` executes a \\\"promote\\\" action, it creates a new task from the inbox item but leaves the original inbox item in the inbox. This requires a separate manual `kspec inbox delete` step to clean up, which is tedious and easy to forget. The inbox item's text is already preserved in the triage record's `item_snapshot` field, so the original is not needed for audit trail purposes.\\n\\n**Where**: \\n- `src/triage/actions.ts` lines 54-74 — the `promote` case in `executeTriageAction()`. Currently creates the task and returns, but never calls `deleteInboxItem()` for the promoted item.\\n- `src/cli/commands/triage.ts` lines 235-289 — the `triage act` command handler. This is where the `--consume` flag would be parsed and passed to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n- The `delete` and `duplicate` cases (lines 77-90, 117-130) already call `deleteInboxItem()` — the promote case can follow the same pattern.\\n\\n**How**: Two reasonable approaches:\\n1. **Flag approach**: Add `--consume` flag to `kspec triage act` that deletes the inbox item after promote. Pass it through `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` to `executeTriageAction()`.\\n2. **Default behavior**: Make deletion the default for promote (since the snapshot is preserved in the triage record), with a `--keep` flag to opt out.\\n\\nRecommendation: Option 2 (delete by default) is cleaner since every promoted item has its text preserved in `item_snapshot`. The `--keep` flag is the escape hatch. The `delete` and `duplicate` cases already delete inbox items as their default behavior, so promote doing the same is consistent.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n1. Add `consume` option to `ExecuteTriageActionOptions` (default true for promote)\\n2. In the `promote` case of `executeTriageAction()`, after creating the task, call `deleteInboxItem()` for `record.inbox_ref` unless consume is false\\n3. Add `--keep` flag to `kspec triage act` command that sets `consume: false`\\n4. Update dry-run output to mention deletion\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- `kspec triage act <ref>` on a promote record creates the task AND deletes the inbox item\\n- `kspec triage act <ref> --keep` creates the task but preserves the inbox item\\n- Dry-run output mentions that the inbox item would be deleted\\n- The `item_snapshot` in the triage record still contains the original text for audit trail\\n- Tests cover: promote with deletion (default), promote with --keep, dry-run output\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1FCWDDH5FM5JKSWNYVBX\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserv\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI: persist all filter state and current view in URL query params so refreshing the page preserves state. Applies to all list/filter pages (triage, inbox, tasks, etc).\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@web-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.004Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQB2J9SKA1QNC3SG3NN8WF\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:17.546Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: Web UI pages lose their filter state when the browser refreshes. For example, filtering tasks by status=in_progress or triage by tag=cli is lost on reload. Filter state should be persisted in URL query parameters so that refreshing preserves the view, and URLs are shareable/bookmarkable.\\n\\n**Where**: The tasks page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/tasks/+page.svelte`) already partially implements URL-based filters — it reads from `$page.url.searchParams` (line 39-47) via the `filterParams` derived value. However, the triage page (`packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) uses local `$state` variables (`filterTag`, `filterStatus` at lines 43-44) that are NOT synced to URL params. Other list pages (items, inbox) likely have the same issue.\\n\\nKey files to update:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte` — filterTag and filterStatus need URL param sync\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/routes/items/+page.svelte` — check if filters use URL params\\n- Any other list/filter pages under `packages/web-ui/src/routes/`\\n\\n**How**: Follow the pattern already established in the tasks page:\\n1. Read initial filter values from `$page.url.searchParams` (from `$app/stores`)\\n2. When a filter changes, use `goto()` (from `$app/navigation`) to update URL params without a full page reload: `goto(\\\\`?status=\\\\${filterStatus}&tag=\\\\${filterTag}\\\\`, { replaceState: true, keepFocus: true })`\\n3. Use `$derived` to compute filter values from URL params (like tasks page does)\\n4. This approach gives: URL persistence, back/forward navigation, shareable links\\n\\nFor the triage page specifically:\\n- Replace `let filterTag = $state('')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('tag')`\\n- Replace `let filterStatus = $state('all')` with reading from `$page.url.searchParams.get('status')`\\n- Add `goto()` calls when filters change\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- All list/filter pages read initial state from URL query params\\n- Changing a filter updates the URL without full page reload\\n- Refreshing the page preserves filter state\\n- Browser back/forward navigates between filter states\\n- The triage page URL shows filters like `?status=untriaged&tag=cli`\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWG9550XY4Y8F7V6W5W47\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:48.645Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @web-ui module. URL state persistence for filter/view params across all list pages.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1G6HBSJRXVSWEWQ9W9E4\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, d\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI triage page: add filter controls for triage action types (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). Currently no way to filter triage records by action.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@interactive-triage-ui\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:03.825Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQBJ4QTDMCZJ4NAY7B1KGP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:33.495Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The triage page has status and tag filters (lines 326-347 of `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`) but no way to filter triage records by their action type (promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate). When reviewing triage history, users often want to see \\\"all promote decisions\\\" or \\\"all spec-gap findings\\\" without scrolling through mixed results.\\n\\n**Where**: `packages/web-ui/src/routes/triage/+page.svelte`:\\n- Existing filters: `filterStatus` (all/untriaged/triaged/acted_on) at line 44, `filterTag` at line 43\\n- Filter UI: lines 324-347 (the filter controls div with data-testid=\\\"triage-filters\\\")\\n- Filtered items computation: `filteredItems` derived at lines 78-96 — currently filters by tag and status only\\n- The `TriageAction` type includes: promote, delete, defer, spec-gap, duplicate (defined in `$lib/types/triage`)\\n\\n**How**:\\n1. Add a new `filterAction` state variable: `let filterAction = $state<TriageAction | ''>('')`\\n2. Add a new `<select>` element in the filters div (alongside status and tag filters) with options for each action type\\n3. Add action filtering to the `filteredItems` derived computation:\\n   ```\\n   if (filterAction) {\\n     items = items.filter((i) => i.record?.action === filterAction);\\n   }\\n   ```\\n4. Include the action filter in the `$effect` that resets `currentIndex` when filters change (line 296-302)\\n5. If implementing URL state persistence (task @01KJDQ1F), also sync this filter to URL params\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- A dropdown/select control for triage action type appears in the filter bar\\n- Selecting \\\"promote\\\" shows only items with promote decisions\\n- Selecting \\\"spec-gap\\\" shows only spec-gap items\\n- The empty/default option shows all items (no action filter)\\n- Changing the filter resets the card navigation index to 0\\n- E2E test covers filtering by action type\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQWRKV7J9BSC3BY2NNPNG8\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:47:57.179Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @interactive-triage-ui spec. Add filter controls for triage action types on triage page.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQ1GZVK77Y8CETXW2E56TG\",\n    \"slugs\": [],\n    \"title\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation\",\n    \"type\": \"task\",\n    \"description\": \"Web UI WebSocket connection unreliable - continually disconnects and reconnects. Needs investigation into reconnection logic, heartbeat handling, or fallback to polling.\",\n    \"spec_ref\": \"@daemon-server\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"blocked_by\": [],\n    \"depends_on\": [],\n    \"context\": [],\n    \"priority\": 3,\n    \"tags\": [],\n    \"vcs_refs\": [],\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:33:04.635Z\",\n    \"notes\": [\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQC6EMCTTQFKDBQX9JCG55\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:38:54.292Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"ENRICHMENT — Standalone context for this task:\\n\\n**What**: The Web UI WebSocket connection continually disconnects and reconnects. The `WebSocketManager` in `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` has exponential backoff reconnection (1s, 2s, 4s... max 30s, max 10 attempts), but users report seeing frequent disconnect/reconnect cycles in the console logs. This suggests either the server is closing connections, the keepalive/heartbeat mechanism is missing, or reconnection resets are happening incorrectly.\\n\\n**Where**:\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/websocket/manager.ts` — The WebSocketManager class. Key areas:\\n  - `connect()` (line 150): creates WebSocket, sets up onopen/onmessage/onerror/onclose handlers\\n  - `scheduleReconnect()` (line 371): exponential backoff logic, max 10 attempts\\n  - `onclose` handler (line 183): triggers reconnect on any close event, even clean ones\\n  - No heartbeat/ping mechanism — if the server expects periodic pings, the connection may time out\\n- `packages/web-ui/src/lib/stores/connection.svelte.ts` — Connection store wrapping the manager\\n- Server-side WebSocket handler (in the daemon code) — check if it sends pings or has idle timeouts\\n\\n**How**: Investigation and fix areas:\\n1. **Add heartbeat/ping**: Implement a periodic ping from client to server (e.g., every 30s). Many WebSocket servers close idle connections after a timeout (often 60s). The client should send a ping command and expect a pong response.\\n2. **Don't reconnect on clean close**: The `onclose` handler (line 183) calls `scheduleReconnect()` unconditionally. If `event.code === 1000` (normal closure) and the client initiated it via `disconnect()`, skip reconnection. Currently `disconnect()` clears the timer, but there's a race if onclose fires after disconnect resets state.\\n3. **Check server-side idle timeout**: The daemon's WebSocket server may have an idle timeout that closes connections. Either increase it or implement heartbeat to keep alive.\\n4. **Consider connection health check**: After reconnecting, verify the connection is actually healthy (e.g., send a ping and wait for response) before transitioning to 'connected' state.\\n5. **Investigate MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS**: After 10 failed reconnects, the connection is abandoned permanently. Consider resetting the attempt counter after a successful connection that lasts longer than some threshold (e.g., 60s).\\n\\n**Done when**:\\n- WebSocket connection remains stable for extended periods without spurious disconnects\\n- Client sends periodic heartbeat/ping to prevent idle timeout\\n- Clean disconnects (code 1000 from client-initiated disconnect) don't trigger reconnection\\n- Console shows clean connection lifecycle without repeated disconnect/reconnect spam\\n- Connection lost indicator (10s threshold) only triggers for genuine server unavailability\\n- Tests cover: heartbeat keeps connection alive, clean close doesn't reconnect, reconnection works after genuine server restart\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"_ulid\": \"01KJDQX0Y1T915X3M23D1VS9PP\",\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-02-26T19:48:05.698Z\",\n        \"author\": \"@claude\",\n        \"content\": \"Linked to @daemon-server spec (covers WebSocket protocol). Investigate and fix WebSocket reconnection reliability.\",\n        \"supersedes\": null\n      }\n    ],\n    \"todos\": [],\n    \"automation\": \"eligible\",\n    \"_sourceFile\": \"/home/chapel/Projects/kynetic-spec/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml\"\n  }\n]\n","status":"completed"}